.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt FANCURVE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fancurve
.Nd temperature-driven fan control for the UGREEN DXP2800
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm service fancurve
.Cm start | stop | restart | status
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl c Ar channel
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl w Ar window
.Op Fl m Ar min
.Op Fl u Ar stepup
.Op Fl s Ar stepdown
.Op Fl C Ar lo : Ns Ar hi
.Op Fl X Ar crit
.Op Fl D Ar lo : Ns Ar hi
.Op Fl d Ar disks
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sets the speed of the system fan from the CPU and disk temperatures,
using
.Xr itefan 8 .
It is normally run by the
.Pa fancurve
.Xr rc 8
service under
.Xr daemon 8 ,
and configured in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.Pp
Every
.Va fancurve_interval
seconds
.Nm
does the following:
.Bl -enum
.It
Read the hottest CPU core from
.Xr coretemp 4
and average it over the last
.Va fancurve_window
seconds, so that short spikes do not change the fan speed much.
.It
Turn the average into a target speed with the CPU curve: at or below
.Ar lo
the target is
.Va fancurve_min ,
at or above
.Ar hi
it is 100%, and in between it rises linearly.
.It
About once a minute, read the hottest disk with
.Xr smartctl 8
and turn it into a target with the disk curve in the same way.
Disks in standby are not woken up.
The higher of the CPU and disk targets is used.
.It
Move the fan towards the target by at most
.Va fancurve_step_up
percent faster, or
.Va fancurve_step_down
percent slower, per interval.
.It
Write the result with
.Ql itefan pct .
The value is written every interval, so a reset by the firmware is
corrected within one interval.
.El
.Pp
Two situations skip the averaging and rate limits and set 100% at once:
a single CPU reading at or above
.Va fancurve_crit ,
and a CPU temperature that cannot be read at all.
.Pp
When
.Nm
stops, for any reason, the fan channel is handed back to the chip's
automatic mode.
The rc script does this as well, even if
.Nm
had already exited.
.Sh RC.CONF VARIABLES
All variables are optional except
.Va fancurve_enable .
Run
.Ql service fancurve restart
after changing them.
.Bl -tag -width "fancurve_step_down"
.It Va fancurve_enable
Set to
.Dq YES
to start
.Nm
at boot.
Default:
.Dq NO .
.It Va fancurve_channel
PWM channel the fan is connected to.
Default: 3.
.It Va fancurve_interval
Seconds between readings.
Default: 10.
.It Va fancurve_window
Seconds over which the CPU temperature is averaged.
Larger values make the fan calmer but slower to react.
Default: 60.
.It Va fancurve_min
Lowest fan speed in percent.
Check that the fan still turns at this value.
Default: 25.
.It Va fancurve_step_up
Largest increase per interval, in percent.
Default: 10.
.It Va fancurve_step_down
Largest decrease per interval, in percent.
Default: 5.
.It Va fancurve_cpu
CPU curve as
.Dq Ar lo : Ns Ar hi
in degrees Celsius.
Default:
.Dq 50:80 .
.It Va fancurve_crit
CPU temperature at which a single reading sets 100% immediately.
Must not be below the
.Ar hi
value of
.Va fancurve_cpu .
Default: 90.
.It Va fancurve_disks
Disks to watch, for example
.Dq ada0 ada1 nvme0 ;
.Dq auto
for all SATA and NVMe disks; empty for none.
Ignored if
.Xr smartctl 8
is not installed.
Default:
.Dq auto .
.It Va fancurve_disk
Disk curve as
.Dq Ar lo : Ns Ar hi
in degrees Celsius.
Default:
.Dq 38:50 .
.It Va fancurve_verbose
Set to
.Dq YES
to log every reading, which helps when tuning.
By default only changes of 10% or more are logged.
Default:
.Dq NO .
.El
.Pp
The command line options set the same values:
.Fl c
channel,
.Fl i
interval,
.Fl w
window,
.Fl m
min,
.Fl u
step up,
.Fl s
step down,
.Fl C
CPU curve,
.Fl X
crit,
.Fl D
disk curve,
.Fl d
disks,
.Fl v
verbose.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/local/etc/rc.d/fancurve" -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/sbin/fancurve
the control loop
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d/fancurve
the rc service script
.It Pa /var/run/fancurve.pid
PID of the supervising
.Xr daemon 8
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Enable the service with the defaults:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysrc kld_list+=coretemp
# sysrc fancurve_enable=YES
# service fancurve start
.Ed
.Pp
A quieter setup that reacts more slowly and never drops below 30%:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysrc fancurve_window=120 fancurve_step_up=5 fancurve_min=30
# service fancurve restart
.Ed
.Pp
Ignore the disks and use only the CPU:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysrc fancurve_disks=""
# service fancurve restart
.Ed
.Pp
Watch what it does:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# sysrc fancurve_verbose=YES
# service fancurve restart
# tail -f /var/log/messages | grep fancurve
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to
.Xr syslog 3
with the tag
.Ql fancurve .
.Bl -diag
.It "cpu 72C (avg 64C) disk 41C -> fan 60%"
Current CPU reading, its average, the hottest disk, and the speed set.
.It "cannot read CPU temperature (coretemp loaded?), fan to 100%"
No CPU temperature is available.
Load
.Xr coretemp 4 .
.It "smartctl not installed, ignoring disks"
Install sysutils/smartmontools to include disk temperatures.
.It "itefan failed to set N%"
.Xr itefan 8
returned an error; run it by hand to see why.
.It "invalid number 'X' in settings"
A value in
.Xr rc.conf 5
is not a number.
.It "stopped, channel N back to automatic"
Normal shutdown.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr coretemp 4 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr daemon 8 ,
.Xr itefan 8 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr smartctl 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An pdillinger@gmail.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Make sure the fan still turns at
.Va fancurve_min ;
some fans stop below a certain duty cycle.
.Pp
Do not change the fan with
.Xr itefan 8
while
.Nm
is running; it overrides manual settings within one interval.
